<p>Compound <b>A</b>, C<sub>5</sub>H<sub>10</sub>O, is one of the basic building blocks of nature. All steroids and many other naturally occurring compounds are built from compound <b>A</b>. Spectroscopic analysis of <b>A</b> yields the following information:</p>

<ul style="list-style-type:none">
    <li class="lt-chem-459829"><span class="&lt;p">IR: 3400 cm<sup>&ndash;1</sup>; 1640 cm<sup>&ndash;1</sup></span></li>
    <li class="lt-chem-459829"><span class="&lt;p"><sup>1</sup>H NMR: 1.63 <i>&delta;</i> (3 H, singlet); 1.70 <i>&delta;</i> (3 H, singlet); 3.83 <i>&delta;</i> (1 H, broad singlet); 4.15 <i>&delta;</i> (2 H, doublet, <i>J</i> = 7 Hz); 5.70 <i>&delta;</i> (1 H, triplet, <i>J</i> = 7 Hz)</span></li>
    <li><span class="&lt;p">(a) </span>How many double bonds and/or rings does <b>A</b> have?</li>
    <li>(b) From the IR spectrum, what is the identity of the oxygen-containing functional group?</li>
    <li>(c) What kinds of hydrogens are responsible for the NMR absorptions listed?</li>
    <li>(d) Propose a structure for <b>A</b>.</li>
</ul>
</div></section>

